p.src:
The inquiry becomes severe when Krishnamurti asks whether we learn from experience. If human beings had learned in the deeper sense, repeated sorrow and repeated war would have ended. Yet the repetition continues. The transcript’s test can be written: $\begin{aligned}
\hbox{repeated sorrow}
&\not\Rightarrow
\hbox{freedom from sorrow},\\
\hbox{repeated war}
&\not\Rightarrow
\hbox{freedom from war}.\end{aligned}$ So the claim is not that experience has no technical value. It is that experience, stored as memory, has not transformed the movement of the mind. It has produced repetition, security, and routine. Education, culture, and civilization have trained the brain to function mechanically.

