p.src:
Krishnamurti then asks whether there is listening out of silence. In that silence there is no wanting, no reward, no punishment, no project of self-improvement. He identifies that listening with attention: $\hbox{silence}+\hbox{listening}
\Rightarrow
\hbox{attention not bound to time}.$ Anderson notices that meditation, in this sense, is not something done in succession. Krishnamurti does not expand the subject here. He says meditation must be gone into deeply another time, partly because the word has been damaged by technique, schedule, payment, and the idea that meditation is something one learns as another acquisition.

